# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Runtime dependencies (direct only) — what the ETL and API actually import.
#
# This file is the SOURCE. requirements.txt is the generated, fully-pinned
# lockfile compiled from it and is what every install (Docker, CI, prod) reads.
# Never hand-edit requirements.txt.
#
# Regenerate after changing anything here:
#     pip install pip-tools
#     pip-compile requirements.in      -o requirements.txt
#     pip-compile requirements-dev.in  -o requirements-dev.txt
#
# CI re-runs both commands on every push and fails if the committed lockfiles
# don't match — see the `deps` job in .github/workflows/ci-cd.yml.
#
# Upper bounds are deliberate, not decoration: each one pins the current major
# so a resolver run can't silently pull a breaking release into a production
# image. Raise a bound consciously, then re-run the compile and the tests.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

